speechtext:
I only rise to remark that I think my hon. friend, the Acting Minister of National Defence (Mr. Macdonald) has overlooked one important point in his argument as to whether this expenditure was or was not authorized under the act as an expenditure which might be made by Sir Henry Thornton and his board of directors, irrespective altogether of the Governor in Council or of the Minister of Railways and Canals (Mr. Graham). Let me point out that when parliament sees fit to make grants for railways or for anything else it usually does so in specific, definite language, surrounded by certain conditions. I note that in the estimate now before the committee out of which this particular $2,000,000 must come, as well as in the similar estimate last year, the language is very definite and very express.
Last year the vote was under three separate headings:	136 for the Canadian National
Railways, 137 for the Grand Trunk Railway Company and 138 for the Grand Trunk Pacific Railway Company. This year the amounts are consolidated under a single item owing to the consolidation of the three railways. But I find that in the three votes last year the limitations, restrictions and conditions were identical with those which accompany this vote to-day. What are they? Under vote 136 last year an amount of $42,000,000 was granted:
(a)	To pay operating deficits;
(b)	Acquisition of property, materials and supplies;
(c)	Interest and sinking funds on notes;
(d)	Principal and interest of maturing or matured loans;
(e)	Construction and betterments;
(f)	Co-ordination or consolidation of railway lines and facilities.
Now, if the section had stopped there, there might have been some force in the argument of the Minister of Railways. But it did not stop there. The next clause is:
The amount herein authorized may be applied from time to time, in the discretion of the Governor in Countcil by way of bonus, in cash or by way of guarantee, or partly one way partly the other.
Supply-Railways-C.N.R.

Money voted by this parliament to this road for the acquisition of property may be expended how? By Sir Henry Thornton? No; at the discretion of the Governor in Council. That is the act passed by the House last year-it is the statute I am reading from; and I find almost identical language in the vote this year. Turn to page 31, vote 139, the one we are now discussing. The language is a little different; in this case the restriction and the limitation are in the first clause. It says:
The amount herein authorized may be applied from time to time, in the discretion of the Governor in Council.
Not in the discretion of the minister; not in the discretion of Sir Henry Thornton. And what is that money for?
